 # Two-Pass GPU Approach
 
-Also called: BT Volume Rendering Pipeline, GPU Ray Casting for Tetrahedral Meshes, GPU-Accelerated Cell Projection (Two-Pass), Tetrahedral Cell Projection, Weiler tetrahedral ray caster
+Also called: BT Volume Rendering Pipeline, CPU/GPU Pipeline for Projected Tetrahedra, GPU Ray Casting for Tetrahedral Meshes, GPU-Accelerated Cell Projection (Two-Pass), Tetrahedral Cell Projection, Volume Rendering Algorithm (CPU/GPU Pipeline), Weiler tetrahedral ray caster
 
 ## Overview
-The Two-Pass GPU Approach is an efficient ray-casting algorithm for tetrahedral meshes implemented on consumer graphics hardware using fragment programs, pre-integration, and multiple rendering passes [S1, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering large unstructured tetrahedral volumes interactively by offloading the geometric projection and classification logic to the GPU, thereby minimizing CPU-GPU data transfer [S3]. Unlike its siblings, it relies on a strict two-stage pipeline: a first pass that computes per-tetrahedron projection data and classification indices in a fragment shader, and a second pass that rasterizes these projections as triangle fans with interpolated optical properties [S4, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from [Hardware-Assisted Projection](5625-hardware-assisted-projection.md) by using explicit fragment shader computations for classification rather than hardware depth-buffer tricks, and from [GPU-Driven Tetrahedral Mesh Rasterization Pipeline](7923-gpu-driven-tetrahedral-mesh-rasterization-pipeline.md) by relying on fixed pipeline stages and CPU-side buffer management rather than fully GPU-driven indirect dispatch [S1, unofficial].
+The Two-Pass GPU Approach is an efficient ray-casting algorithm for tetrahedral meshes implemented on consumer graphics hardware using fragment programs, pre-integration, and multiple rendering passes [S1, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering large unstructured tetrahedral volumes interactively by offloading the geometric projection and classification logic to the GPU, thereby minimizing CPU-GPU data transfer [S3]. The method relies on a strict two-stage pipeline: a first pass that computes per-tetrahedron projection data and classification indices in a fragment shader, and a second pass that rasterizes these projections as triangle fans with interpolated optical properties [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
