 ### Pure Emissive Rendering
-A rendering technique that simply adds the contribution of each tetrahedron [S5]. It uses a 2D texture map since the dependency of the color contribution on the length of the ray segment is linear and can be integrated by modulating the result of the shading lookup with the result of the minimum texture lookup [S5]. It uses the register combiners extension to multiply the result of texture stage 2 with the r coordinate of texture stage 0 [S5]. The texture map in stage 2 contains the integral of the transfer function for the interval $[\min(s_f, s_b), \max(s_f, s_b)]$ at position $(s_f, s_b)$ [S5].
+A rendering technique that simply adds the contribution of each tetrahedron [S6]. It uses a 2D texture map since the dependency of the color contribution on the length of the ray segment is linear and can be integrated by modulating the result of the shading lookup with the result of the minimum texture lookup [S6]. It uses the register combiners extension to multiply the result of texture stage 2 with the r coordinate of texture stage 0 [S6]. The texture map in stage 2 contains the integral of the transfer function for the interval $[\min(s_f, s_b), \max(s_f, s_b)]$ at position $(s_f, s_b)$ [S6].
 
 ### Rendering Algorithm (2nd Order)
-A variant that projects front and back facing quadrilaterals of the bounding box to two distinct 32-bit floating-point auxiliary buffers using a fragment shader that writes interpolated 3D texture coordinates [S6]. It uses the OpenGL extension `GL_ARB_draw_buffers` for rendering multiple targets [S6].
